  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nOverview.\n\nHigh pressure will dominate central Indiana weather into early next \nweek, driving a trend toward clearer skies and warmer temperatures. \nLow clouds will erode through Saturday morning, giving way to \nabundant sunshine and daytime highs reaching the lower 80s by \nMonday. A cold front will approach from the northwest late Tuesday, \nbringing the next likelihood of showers and thunderstorms before \ntemperatures cool back toward seasonal averages late in the week.\n\nRest of Today through Sunday\n\nSurface high pressure is building southwestward with lingering mid-\nlevel cloud cover dissipating as of 3am Saturday. Continued clearing \nis expected as large-scale subsidence from the high takes hold. A \nsubsident patter continues through the weekend as Indiana is between \ntwo systems...one just to our west over the Plains and a nor'easter \nover the Mid Atlantic.\n\nOverall, clear skies will allow daytime temperatures to trend above \nguidance with highs in the 70s. Lows on the other hand will be a bit \nbelow guidance due to efficient radiational cooling conditions. Low \ntemperatures in the 40s to near 50 are expected each night. \nAdditionally, these conditions are good for fog formation and patchy \nfog is possible both Saturday, Sunday, and Monday nights.\n\nMonday Through Friday.\n\nThe extended period opens with weak upper-level ridge amplification \nacross the eastern CONUS as surface high pressure shifts toward the \nMid-Atlantic coast. Both deterministic GFS and ECMWF along with \ntheir respective ensemble suites depict strong low-level warm air \nadvection developing across the Mississippi and Ohio Valleys as the \nlow off the East Coast finally begins to push off to the northeast.\n\nSoutherly flow early next week doesn't look quite as robust as it \ndid in recent model runs which will keep highs more towards the mid \n70s vs lower 80s. This begins to shift towards Wednesday as the \nsynoptic pattern begins to shift. Late Wednesday into Thursday a \nprogressive upper-level trough traverses the Northern Plains and \nGreat Lakes. Deterministic guidance indicates a surface cyclone \ntracking northeast into the Upper Midwest, pulling a trailing cold \nfront toward central Indiana late Wednesday into Thursday which is \nabout 12-18 hours slower than previous model runs. \n\nStrong synoptic-scale forcing via PVA and a developing 35-45 kt low-\nlevel jet will foster moisture transport, elevating PWAT values \ntoward 1.50 inches. Ensemble consensus supports increasing PoPs \nbeginning late Wednesday for showers and scattered thunderstorms \nalong with a lower-end potential for a few strong to severe storms. \nPost-frontal cold air advection behind the frontal passage will \nbreak the warm trend, returning temperatures to near seasonal \nnormals in the upper 60s and lower 70s going into Friday.",
